               |
               v
       Record Email Activity

How It Works

1. Scheduled Execution

The workflow is configured to run daily at 9:00 AM, allowing invoice records to be checked automatically without manual intervention.

2. Invoice Data Retrieval

Zapier accesses the Google Sheets document named P-0 and retrieves the available invoice records.

The workflow uses fields such as:

Invoice ID

Invoice Type

Customer Name

Amount

Account Number

Bank Name

Payment Status

Transaction ID

Reminder Count

3. Conditional Filtering

The retrieved records are evaluated to identify invoices whose Status is exactly Pending.

Records marked Completed, Paid, or with another status are excluded from the first-reminder process.

The workflow also checks that a valid, authorized recipient is available before attempting to send an email.

4. Recipient Selection

The recipient is determined by the invoice Type field:

Maintenance: Route the reminder to the configured A1 recipient.

Project: Route the reminder to the configured A2 recipient.

Only explicitly configured recipients are eligible to receive emails.

5. Personalized Email Generation

For each eligible invoice, Zapier maps the relevant spreadsheet values into a payment reminder email.

The message includes the invoice number, pending amount, payment type, and available banking information. This dynamic mapping allows the same workflow to handle multiple invoices without manually composing each message.

6. Spreadsheet Update

After an email is successfully sent, Zapier updates the relevant invoice record to reflect the reminder activity.

Depending on the workflow configuration, this can include updating the reminder count, recording a timestamp, and logging the sending result.

Only the intended fields should be modified, preserving unrelated invoice information.

Extended Workflow Capabilities

The project can incorporate additional automation stages to support more complete invoice follow-up.

Payment Confirmation Tracking

Gmail can be monitored for relevant replies confirming successful payment. After an authorized confirmation is matched to the correct invoice, the corresponding Status field can be updated from Pending to Completed.

Transaction ID Management

Verified transaction references can be stored against their matching invoice records. Transaction IDs must come from a reliable source and must not be generated arbitrarily.

Second Reminder Automation

Invoices that remain pending more than 24 hours after the first reminder can be selected for a second follow-up.

Eligibility can be based on the invoice status, reminder count, transaction ID availability, escalation flag, and first-reminder timestamp. The second email can use HTML formatting to highlight overdue notices and escalation warnings in red.

Key Features

Scheduled invoice monitoring

Automated retrieval of spreadsheet records

Status-based filtering

Invoice-type-based email routing

Dynamic email content generation

Gmail integration

Automated spreadsheet updates

Reminder count and timestamp tracking

Payment confirmation processing

Duplicate-reminder prevention

Execution logging and error handling

Expected Outcomes

The system reduces manual effort in routine invoice follow-ups and establishes a structured process for tracking outstanding payments.

It demonstrates how low-code automation can connect spreadsheet-based business data with email communication, conditional processing, and record updates.

Important Notes

Configure the actual authorized email addresses for A1 and A2 before running the workflow.

Connect Google Drive, Google Sheets, and Gmail using authorized accounts.

Test the workflow with sample invoice records before enabling automatic execution.

Verify that email sending and spreadsheet updates succeed before marking a reminder as sent.

Treat payment confirmation as a separate verification step rather than assuming that an email reply automatically proves payment.

This project demonstrates an automation workflow; it does not independently verify bank transactions unless a verified payment data source is integrated.

Conclusion

The Automated Payment Reminder System demonstrates the practical application of workflow automation to invoice management. By integrating Zapier with Google Sheets and Gmail, it provides a repeatable approach to pending-payment identification, personalized reminders, payment-status tracking, and follow-up communication.

The project illustrates how low-code automation can improve operational efficiency, reduce repetitive administrative tasks, and support more consistent financial record management.
